--!strict
--!optimize 2
--!lint ConstLocal

const .{
    elements.{ Heading, Paragraph },
    components.{ Code },
    site.{ Page, TableOfContents },
} = require("@chimera")
const .{ h1, h2 } = Heading

return Page("Performance Innovations", {
    h1("Performance Innovations"),
    Paragraph("How Luwu makes classes fast, and when each optimization applies."),
    TableOfContents(),

    h2("Inline fields"),
    Paragraph({
        "An object stores its fields inline, in an order its class fixes up front, ",
        "instead of in a hash table that grows as keys are added. ",
        "That makes objects smaller too: an object with two number fields takes 88 bytes, ",
        "where a table holding the same fields takes 128.",
    }),

    h2("Constant-offset field access"),
    Paragraph({
        "Because the order is fixed, the compiler turns a field access into a constant offset. ",
        "Inside a method, ", Code("self.health"), " compiles to a ", Code("GETOBJECTMEMBER"),
        " instruction (", Code("SETOBJECTMEMBER"), " for writes) that goes straight to the field's slot, ",
        "with no key lookup and no cache to check. ",
        "With native code generation, a field read takes about 0.9 nanoseconds against 2.0 for a table, ",
        "and a write about 0.3 against 4.5. ",
        "Reading the same field twice in a row costs one read, since constant propagation reuses the first.",
    }),

    h2("Proving what self is"),
    Paragraph({
        "The compiler only takes these shortcuts when it can prove what ", Code("self"), " is. ",
        "Every method starts with a self check that raises an error when it's called on the wrong kind of value. ",
        "Past that check, ", Code("self"), " is proven to be an object of the method's class, ",
        "as long as the method never reassigns it. ",
        "Type annotations deliberately don't count as proof, because an annotation can be wrong, ",
        "and a wrong guess here would read the wrong memory instead of failing with an error.",
    }),

    h2("Method inlining"),
    Paragraph({
        "At optimization level 2 (", Code("--!optimize 2"), "), Luwu also inlines method calls. ",
        "The compiler copies the body of a small method into its caller and removes the call entirely. ",
        "This is where most of the speed of classes comes from. ",
        "Inlining follows the same rule of proof: a call inlines when the compiler can prove the receiver's class. ",
        "That covers ", Code("self:method()"), " inside a class, an object the same code just constructed, ",
        "and a value checked with ", Code("class.isinstance"), " in an ", Code("if"), " or an ", Code("assert"), ". ",
        "An inlined body still runs its self check, and still fails loudly on the wrong kind of object. ",
        "Hot code runs fastest in the same module as the classes it uses, since methods only inline within a module.",
    }),

    h2("Trusting type annotations"),
    Paragraph({
        "Proving every receiver gets tedious once objects hold other objects. ",
        "The ", Code("--!trust"), " directive lets a file trust its type annotations instead: ",
        "a parameter, local or field annotated with a class is then enough to pick the method to inline. ",
        "Trust never lets wrong code run quietly, ",
        "because the inlined self check turns a lying annotation into a runtime error. ",
        Code("--!trust"), " is still experimental, and isn't turned on by default yet.",
    }),

    h2("Construction and type tests"),
    Paragraph({
        "Building and testing objects is cheap too. ",
        "Constructing an object compiles to a single ", Code("NEWOBJECT"), " instruction, ",
        "with no temporary table and no function call. ",
        Code("class.isinstance"), " compiles to one fused test-and-branch instruction, ",
        "which native code drops entirely when the class is already known.",
    }),
}, "performance-innovations.html")
